export const DATA_56: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-072", num: 72, title: "Đề 072", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Impact of Wildfires on Forest Ecosystems", paragraphs: ["Wildfires are one of the most powerful and dramatic natural events on Earth. Every year, fires sweep through forests, grasslands, and other wild areas across the globe, burning millions of acres of land. While many people think of wildfires only as disasters that destroy trees and harm wildlife, the relationship between fire and forest ecosystems is actually much more complex. In many cases, wildfires play an important and even necessary role in maintaining the health and balance of natural environments.", "For thousands of years, wildfires have been a natural part of forest life. They are often started by lightning strikes during dry seasons, when dead leaves, fallen branches, and dry grass provide plenty of fuel. Some forests, such as the pine forests of North America and the eucalyptus forests of Australia, have evolved over long periods of time to depend on fire. The trees in these forests have developed special features that help them survive fires or even benefit from them. For example, some types of pine trees have cones that only open and release their seeds after being exposed to the intense heat of a fire. This allows new trees to grow quickly in the open, sunlit ground left behind after a fire has passed.", "One of the most important roles that fire plays in forest ecosystems is clearing away old and dead plant material. Over time, dead wood, dry leaves, and thick layers of undergrowth can build up on the forest floor. This buildup can block sunlight from reaching younger plants and create conditions that favor disease and pest infestations. When a fire burns through and removes this material, it allows light to reach the soil and gives new plants the space and nutrients they need to grow. Many wildflowers and grasses are among the first species to reappear after a fire, often in greater numbers than before, because the cleared ground and enriched soil create ideal conditions for their growth.", "Wildfires also have important effects on animal life. While it is true that some animals are harmed or killed during a fire, many species have adapted to cope with wildfires and even rely on them for their survival. Woodpeckers, for instance, are known to follow the smoke of wildfires because burned trees attract the insects that these birds feed on. Deer, rabbits, and other grazing animals often benefit from the fresh, nutritious grass and young plants that grow quickly after a fire. Some bird species even prefer to build their nests in burned areas because the open landscape offers better visibility and fewer hiding places for predators.", "Despite the natural benefits of fire, not all wildfires are helpful. When fires burn too frequently, too intensely, or in areas where they do not naturally occur, they can cause serious and long-lasting damage. Soil erosion is one major concern: after a fire removes the protective layer of plants and roots, heavy rains can wash away the topsoil, making it difficult for forests to recover. Air quality is also severely affected during large wildfires, as smoke and ash spread over wide areas, creating health problems for both humans and animals. In some regions, wildfires have become more frequent and more destructive due to climate change, which is causing hotter temperatures, longer dry seasons, and stronger winds that help fires spread more rapidly.", "Scientists and forest managers have developed a number of strategies to reduce the risk of destructive wildfires while preserving the natural role of fire in ecosystems. One widely used approach is the practice of controlled burning, also known as prescribed fire. In this method, land managers deliberately set small, carefully managed fires during cooler and wetter conditions to clear away excess dead material before it can fuel a much larger and more dangerous wildfire. Indigenous communities in many parts of the world have practiced this technique for centuries as a way of managing the land. Today, forest managers in countries such as the United States, Canada, and Australia rely on controlled burns as an essential tool for protecting both forests and nearby human communities.", "Understanding the role of wildfires in nature is an important step toward managing them more wisely. Rather than viewing every wildfire as a catastrophe to be stopped at all costs, scientists and policymakers are increasingly recognizing that some degree of fire is both natural and necessary for healthy forest ecosystems. By learning to work with fire rather than simply against it, we can better protect our forests, our communities, and the remarkable diversity of life that depends on these vital natural spaces."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Controlled burning is the most effective method ever developed for protecting forests from large wildfires.", "Wildfires play a complex role in forest ecosystems, offering natural benefits while also posing serious risks when they occur excessively.", "Wildfires are entirely destructive events that must be prevented through modern firefighting technology.", "Climate change is the primary cause of wildfires and must be addressed before any forest management strategy can succeed."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the pine forests of North America and the eucalyptus forests of Australia", "the dead leaves and fallen branches that fuel wildfires", "the dry seasons when wildfires most commonly occur", "the lightning strikes that start natural fires in wild areas"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do some pine trees benefit from wildfires?", options: ["Their leaves produce a chemical that prevents fire from spreading to nearby trees.", "Their cones only open and release seeds after being exposed to the intense heat of a fire.", "Their bark becomes thicker after repeated exposure to fire, protecting them from future burns.", "Their roots absorb nutrients from the ash left behind after a fire, helping them grow faster."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happens to the forest floor when dead plant material builds up over time?", options: ["It forms a protective layer that prevents soil erosion during heavy rainstorms.", "It attracts a wider variety of animal species, which helps maintain biodiversity.", "It breaks down naturally over time, releasing nutrients that benefit mature trees.", "It creates conditions that can block sunlight from younger plants and encourage disease and pest infestations."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do woodpeckers follow the smoke of wildfires?", options: ["They use the heat from fires to warm their nests during colder months.", "They migrate toward fire-affected areas to avoid competition from other bird species.", "They are attracted to the open landscape that makes hunting easier after a fire.", "They follow fires because burned trees attract the insects that woodpeckers feed on."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"reappear\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["spread rapidly into new and unfamiliar areas", "come back into existence after being absent", "compete aggressively with other plant species", "survive without any water or nutrients"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the purpose of controlled burning, or prescribed fire?", options: ["To clear away excess dead material during safer conditions before it can fuel a larger and more dangerous wildfire.", "To create open land for agricultural use in areas where forests have become too dense.", "To deliberately destroy old-growth forests and replace them with faster-growing tree species.", "To test the effectiveness of new firefighting equipment in real forest conditions."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a negative consequence of wildfires?", options: ["The permanent extinction of rare plant species found only in fire-prone forests", "Increased frequency and intensity of wildfires due to climate change", "Air quality problems from smoke and ash spreading over wide areas", "Soil erosion caused by heavy rain washing away topsoil after fires remove plant cover"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"deliberately\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["cautiously and with great physical effort", "quickly and without proper planning", "on purpose and with a clear intention", "accidentally and without any preparation"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the author's view of wildfire management?", options: ["The author suggests that a better understanding of fire's natural role can lead to more effective and balanced forest management.", "The author argues that scientists should stop studying wildfires and focus instead on preventing climate change.", "The author implies that wildfires are too unpredictable to be managed effectively by any human strategy.", "The author believes that all wildfires should be extinguished as quickly as possible to protect human communities."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Global Challenge of Plastic Pollution", paragraphs: ["Over the past century, plastic has become one of the most widely used materials in human history. Lightweight, durable, cheap to produce, and adaptable to an enormous variety of applications, plastic transformed industries from packaging and construction to medicine and technology. Today, the world produces more than 400 million tonnes of plastic every year. Yet this remarkable material has also created one of the most pressing environmental challenges of the modern era: plastic pollution. The vast majority of plastic ever produced has not been recycled or safely disposed of, and a significant portion has found its way into the natural environment, where it persists for centuries.", "One of the most visible and troubling dimensions of plastic pollution is its impact on the world's oceans. An estimated eight million tonnes of plastic enter the ocean every year, where currents and winds gradually concentrate it into vast accumulations. The largest of these, often referred to as the Great Pacific Garbage Patch, spans an area roughly three times the size of France in the North Pacific Ocean. Much of this material is not the large, easily visible plastic items that people associate with pollution, but rather tiny fragments known as microplastics — pieces smaller than five millimeters in size that result from the gradual breakdown of larger plastic objects under the effects of sunlight and wave action. These particles have now been detected in virtually every corner of the ocean, from the surface to the deepest known trenches.", "The consequences of marine plastic pollution for ocean ecosystems are profound . Sea turtles frequently mistake plastic bags for jellyfish, one of their primary food sources, and consume them in fatal quantities. Seabirds such as albatrosses feed on floating plastic debris and carry it back to their nests, inadvertently poisoning their chicks. Whales, dolphins, and other large marine mammals are found stranded on beaches with stomachs filled with plastic bags, fishing nets, and other discarded materials. Beyond these dramatic cases, microplastics pose a more subtle but pervasive threat: they are ingested by the tiny marine organisms at the base of the food chain, and the toxic chemicals they carry accumulate in increasing concentrations as they pass up through successive levels of the food web — a process known as bioaccumulation .", "Plastic pollution is not confined to the ocean. Studies have found microplastics in rivers, lakes, soil, and even in the air of major cities. Alarmingly, microplastics have been detected in human blood, lung tissue, and breast milk, raising urgent questions about the long-term health effects of chronic exposure to plastic particles. While scientists are still working to understand the full extent of these effects, laboratory studies have linked certain plastic-associated chemicals, such as bisphenol A and various phthalates, to hormonal disruption, developmental problems in children, and increased risk of certain cancers. The true scale of plastic's infiltration into living systems — including human bodies — is only beginning to be understood.", "Addressing plastic pollution requires action at multiple levels. At the individual level, reducing single-use plastic consumption — avoiding disposable cups, straws, bags, and packaging where alternatives exist — represents a meaningful but ultimately insufficient response on its own. At the corporate level, pressure from consumers and regulators has led some companies to commit to using more recycled materials, redesigning packaging to reduce plastic content, and investing in take-back programs that allow products to be returned at the end of their useful life. At the government level, a growing number of countries have introduced bans or restrictions on specific types of single-use plastic, though the effectiveness of these measures varies significantly depending on enforcement capacity and the availability of affordable alternatives.", "Perhaps the most significant development in the global response to plastic pollution has been the negotiation of a legally binding international treaty on plastic pollution under the auspices of the United Nations. Discussions that began in earnest in 2022 have aimed to establish shared obligations for reducing plastic production, improving waste management, and holding producers financially responsible for the end-of-life costs of the plastic they manufacture — a concept known as extended producer responsibility. Reaching agreement on the treaty's scope and ambition has proved contentious, with major plastic-producing nations resisting provisions that would limit production volumes, while environmentally focused countries and advocacy groups have pushed for comprehensive and enforceable commitments.", "The story of plastic pollution is, in many respects, a story about the gap between short-term convenience and long-term consequence. Plastic became indispensable precisely because it solved immediate practical problems so elegantly: it was cheaper, lighter, and more versatile than almost any material that preceded it. The costs of that convenience, however, were diffuse and deferred — distributed across ecosystems, species, and human bodies in ways that only became apparent generations after the material's widespread adoption. Addressing those costs will require not merely better technology or stronger regulation, but a fundamental rethinking of how societies design, produce, and manage materials across the full arc of their useful life."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To explain why recycling programs have failed to solve the problem of plastic waste in developed countries.", "To examine the origins, environmental and health impacts, and global responses to plastic pollution.", "To argue that plastic should be completely banned from all industrial and commercial applications worldwide.", "To compare the effectiveness of different national policies on single-use plastic bans."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"profound\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["sudden and completely unexpected", "temporary and easily reversible", "largely invisible and difficult to study", "deep, serious, and far-reaching"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the Great Pacific Garbage Patch primarily consist of?", options: ["Tiny plastic fragments known as microplastics, which result from the breakdown of larger plastic objects.", "A mixture of plastic and other non-biodegradable materials such as metal and glass.", "Industrial plastic waste dumped directly into the ocean by manufacturing companies.", "Large, easily visible plastic items such as bottles and containers discarded from ships."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is \"bioaccumulation\" as described in the passage?", options: ["The ability of certain marine organisms to break down plastic into non-toxic components.", "The tendency of ocean currents to concentrate plastic debris into large patches.", "The process by which plastic particles dissolve harmlessly in seawater over time.", "The process by which toxic chemicals carried by microplastics increase in concentration as they move up the food chain."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which health concern has been linked to certain chemicals associated with plastic?", options: ["Reduced immune function in adults who consume large quantities of seafood", "Hormonal disruption and developmental problems in children", "Damage to the nervous system resulting from prolonged contact with plastic containers", "Increased risk of respiratory infections caused by airborne plastic fibers"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"accumulate\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["attach permanently to the surface of living organisms", "build up gradually in increasing amounts", "spread outward evenly across a wide area", "break apart into smaller and less harmful pieces"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does \"extended producer responsibility\" refer to?", options: ["An agreement between companies to share the costs of cleaning up ocean plastic collectively.", "A regulation that limits the total amount of plastic a single company is allowed to produce each year.", "A concept that holds manufacturers financially accountable for the end-of-life costs of the plastic they produce.", "A policy that requires consumers to pay extra fees when purchasing products made from virgin plastic."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a corporate-level response to plastic pollution?", options: ["Funding independent scientific research into the health effects of microplastics", "Redesigning packaging to reduce plastic content", "Committing to using more recycled materials in products", "Investing in take-back programs for products at the end of their useful life"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about why plastic pollution was not addressed sooner?", options: ["Governments deliberately concealed evidence of plastic pollution to protect the interests of major manufacturers.", "The benefits of plastic were immediate and obvious, while the environmental and health costs emerged slowly across ecosystems and generations.", "Scientists lacked the equipment needed to detect microplastics in the environment until recently.", "Most people were aware of plastic's dangers but chose to ignore them because affordable alternatives did not exist."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following best describes the author's view of how plastic pollution should ultimately be addressed?", options: ["Individual behavioral change is the most powerful tool available and should be the primary focus of any solution.", "International treaty negotiations are the only meaningful path forward and should proceed without compromise.", "Advances in recycling technology alone will be sufficient to resolve the plastic pollution crisis within the next decade.", "A comprehensive rethinking of how societies design, produce, and manage materials throughout their entire life cycle is required."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Understanding Burnout in Generation Z", paragraphs: ["Generation Z — broadly defined as those born between the mid-1990s and early 2010s — has entered adulthood during one of the most turbulent periods in recent history. Coming of age amid a global pandemic, an escalating climate crisis, rising economic inequality, and the relentless pressures of a hyperconnected digital world, members of this generation face a constellation of stressors with few clear precedents. It is perhaps unsurprising, then, that rates of burnout among young people have reached levels that researchers and mental health professionals are describing as a public health concern. What is less immediately obvious is why burnout appears to affect this generation differently — and more severely — than older cohorts who have faced their own share of hardship.", "Burnout, as defined by the World Health Organization, is a syndrome resulting from chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed. [1] Its hallmarks are exhaustion, increasing mental distance from one's job, and reduced professional efficacy. While the term was originally applied to working adults in high-demand professions, researchers have increasingly noted its appearance among students and young people who have not yet entered the formal workforce. Academic burnout — characterized by emotional exhaustion, cynicism toward schoolwork, and a sense of personal inadequacy — mirrors its occupational counterpart closely enough that many psychologists now treat them as variations of the same underlying phenomenon. The extension of burnout frameworks to educational settings has allowed researchers to identify patterns of chronic stress in adolescents that might otherwise have been attributed to ordinary laziness or lack of motivation.", "Several structural features of Generation Z's developmental environment appear to have intensified the conditions under which burnout takes hold. The first, and perhaps most distinctive, is the pervasive influence of social media. Unlike previous generations, who experienced adolescence largely offline, members of Generation Z have grown up in an environment of continuous social comparison, curated self-presentation, and constant availability to peers and, increasingly, professional networks. Research has consistently linked heavy social media use to elevated levels of anxiety, reduced self-esteem, and sleep disturbances — each of which constitutes an independent risk factor for burnout. The pressure to maintain a visible, polished, and productive digital presence does not disappear after school hours but extends into evenings and weekends, effectively eliminating the recovery time that psychological research identifies as essential for preventing chronic stress from becoming burnout. [2]", "Economic precarity represents a second major structural driver. Generation Z has come of age in an era of inflated housing costs, stagnant entry-level wages relative to living expenses, and labor markets increasingly characterized by short-term contracts, gig work, and diminished job security. Unlike their parents, many of whom could reasonably expect that a university degree would translate into stable, well-compensated employment, young people today frequently find themselves overqualified for available positions while simultaneously burdened by significant student debt. This gap between aspiration and economic reality — between what was promised and what is actually on offer — generates a particular form of exhaustion that researchers have termed \"striving fatigue\": the depletion that results not from failure to try, but from trying persistently in a system that appears structurally indifferent to effort. [3]", "The role of the COVID-19 pandemic in shaping Generation Z's relationship with work and wellbeing deserves particular attention. For many members of this generation, the pandemic coincided with critical developmental transitions: finishing school, beginning university, entering the workforce, or establishing independent living arrangements. The abrupt disruption of these milestones — combined with prolonged periods of social isolation, grief, and uncertainty — left psychological traces that researchers are only beginning to document. Surveys conducted in the years following the pandemic's peak consistently find that young people who experienced significant disruption during this period report higher baseline levels of anxiety, greater difficulty maintaining motivation, and a more fragile relationship with professional identity than comparable cohorts who did not face the same degree of disruption.", "Responses to the burnout crisis among Generation Z have emerged across several domains. Within workplaces, a small but growing number of employers have begun to experiment with four-day work weeks, mandatory disconnect policies, and mental health days as structural accommodations rather than exceptional benefits. In educational settings, universities and secondary schools have introduced wellbeing programs, reduced assessment loads, and expanded access to counseling services. [4] At the policy level, some governments have begun to acknowledge that addressing burnout requires more than individual coping strategies — that the structural conditions producing chronic stress must themselves be reformed through measures such as strengthening labor protections, expanding access to affordable housing, and reducing the financial burden of higher education.", "What distinguishes the burnout conversation among Generation Z from earlier discussions of workplace stress is the degree to which young people themselves have driven it into public consciousness. Through social media — the same medium implicated in producing much of their distress — members of this generation have articulated their experiences with a candor and precision that has forced employers, educators, and policymakers to take the issue seriously in ways they might not have done otherwise. Whether this heightened visibility translates into meaningful structural reform, or whether it remains largely a matter of individual resilience and private coping, will likely define not just the wellbeing of this generation but the broader relationship between work, identity, and human flourishing in the decades to come."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of the passage?", options: ["The passage focuses primarily on how the COVID-19 pandemic disrupted the educational experiences of young people worldwide.", "The passage criticizes employers and universities for failing to provide adequate mental health support to young workers and students.", "The passage argues that Generation Z is less resilient than previous generations due to overuse of social media platforms.", "The passage examines the causes, characteristics, and societal responses to the elevated levels of burnout experienced by Generation Z."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is identified by the World Health Organization as a hallmark of burnout?", options: ["Frequent physical illness resulting from a weakened immune system under chronic stress", "A sudden loss of interest in hobbies and personal relationships outside of work", "An inability to concentrate for more than short periods due to information overload", "Increasing mental distance from one's job alongside reduced professional efficacy"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on information from both paragraphs 2 and 3, why is burnout now considered a concern even for young people who have not yet entered the workforce?", options: ["Because Generation Z members have generally chosen more demanding academic programs than previous generations, increasing their overall stress load.", "Because academic burnout mirrors occupational burnout closely, and social media creates conditions of chronic stress that replicate workplace pressures during non-school hours.", "Because academic institutions now impose the same performance metrics on students that corporations use for professional employees.", "Because the formal definition of burnout was recently revised by the WHO to include stress experienced in any structured environment, not just workplaces."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the term \"striving fatigue\" refer to?", options: ["The physical exhaustion experienced by young people who take on multiple part-time jobs simultaneously", "The depletion that results from persistent effort in a system that appears structurally indifferent to that effort", "The sense of failure that develops when young people compare their achievements to those of their parents' generation", "The psychological burnout caused by setting unrealistically high personal goals during early adulthood"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following did surveys find among young people who experienced significant disruption during the pandemic?", options: ["They were more likely to change careers multiple times in the years following the pandemic.", "They showed stronger social bonds with peers as a result of shared adversity during lockdowns.", "They reported higher baseline levels of anxiety and greater difficulty maintaining motivation.", "They were more financially successful than comparable cohorts due to increased savings during the lockdown period."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a workplace response to burnout among young employees?", options: ["Four-day work weeks", "Mandatory disconnect policies", "Mental health days as structural accommodations", "Performance bonuses linked to wellbeing metrics"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What conclusion can be drawn by combining information from paragraphs 4 and 6 about the most effective approach to addressing burnout in Generation Z?", options: ["Individual coping strategies and personal resilience training are the most practical tools available, given the difficulty of reforming economic systems.", "Burnout can be most effectively reduced by encouraging young people to lower their career aspirations to match the realities of the current labor market.", "Addressing burnout requires structural reforms to conditions such as labor protections, housing affordability, and educational costs, not just individual or workplace-level interventions.", "The primary responsibility for addressing burnout lies with universities, which must reduce academic pressure before workplace reforms can have any meaningful effect."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["academic burnout and occupational burnout", "adolescents who have been misdiagnosed with motivational disorders", "high-demand professions and corporate workplaces", "psychologists who specialize in treating workplace stress disorders"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"structurally indifferent\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["increasingly unstable due to rapid changes in global economic conditions", "deliberately designed to reward only those with exceptional talent", "organized around values that prioritize collective welfare over personal ambition", "built in a way that does not respond or adapt to individual effort"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to the passage? \"This chronic accumulation of stress, with no clear boundary between 'on' and 'off' time, is precisely the pattern that clinical research identifies as most likely to produce burnout over extended periods.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Artificial Intelligence and the Future of Human Labor", paragraphs: ["The relationship between technological innovation and human employment has never been straightforward. Each major wave of automation — from the mechanization of agriculture in the eighteenth century to the computerization of clerical work in the twentieth — has generated both profound disruption and, in the longer run, the emergence of new categories of work that earlier observers could not have predicted. [1] The current wave of artificial intelligence, however, presents features that distinguish it from its predecessors in ways that complicate the reassuring historical narrative: unlike previous automation technologies, which were largely confined to physically repetitive or computationally bounded tasks, contemporary AI systems have demonstrated a capacity to perform complex cognitive functions — pattern recognition, language generation, legal reasoning, medical diagnosis — that were until recently considered the exclusive province of trained human professionals.", "The economic literature on AI's labor market effects is extensive, contested, and rapidly evolving. Early influential studies projected that a substantial proportion of existing occupations — some analyses suggested figures approaching forty-seven percent of all jobs in developed economies — were at high risk of automation within the following two decades. Subsequent research challenged these projections as overly deterministic, arguing that automation risk assessments based on task composition systematically underestimate the importance of social, creative, and contextual dimensions of work that AI systems struggle to replicate. The emerging consensus, to the extent that one exists, holds that AI is more likely to augment human workers in most occupations than to replace them wholesale, while simultaneously restructuring the skill requirements and economic returns associated with a wide range of roles. [2]", "This restructuring is already underway in ways both visible and subtle. In knowledge-intensive industries such as law, finance, and medicine, AI tools are being deployed to assist professionals with tasks that once consumed significant portions of their working time: reviewing contracts, screening diagnostic images, flagging anomalous transactions. The effect, in many cases, is not the elimination of professional roles but a compression of the time required to perform certain functions — freeing practitioners to focus on higher-order judgment, client relationship management, and the kinds of nuanced decision-making that current AI systems handle poorly. Yet this compression simultaneously reduces the volume of junior-level work that has historically served as the training ground through which entry-level professionals develop the tacit knowledge and contextual judgment that senior practice demands. [3]", "The distributional consequences of AI-driven labor market restructuring are likely to be highly unequal, and this inequality operates along multiple dimensions simultaneously. Workers in occupations requiring the manipulation of physical environments — plumbers, electricians, care workers, construction laborers — face significantly lower near-term automation risk than many office-based roles, a reversal of the historical pattern in which blue-collar labor was displaced first and most severely. At the same time, workers in lower-wage service occupations requiring routine cognitive tasks — data entry, basic customer service, document processing — face more immediate displacement pressure than higher-wage professionals whose work involves complex judgment and interpersonal dynamics. The result is a potential polarization of the labor market: a hollowing out of the middle-skill, middle-wage occupations that have historically constituted the backbone of the middle class in developed economies.", "The governance of AI's labor market effects has become an increasingly urgent preoccupation for policymakers, though the policy toolkit available to governments is constrained by the speed of technological change and the transnational nature of the platforms and organizations driving it. Proposals range from incremental adjustments to existing labor market institutions — strengthening unemployment insurance, expanding retraining programs, extending portable benefits to gig workers — to more radical structural interventions such as universal basic income, robot taxes levied on firms that automate previously human-performed work, and the mandatory sharing of AI-generated productivity gains with affected workers through profit-sharing schemes. Each of these proposals carries its own distributional logic, political constituency, and set of unresolved empirical questions about behavioral effects and fiscal sustainability.", "What is perhaps most challenging about the AI transition is not any single policy question but the pace at which the technology is advancing relative to the capacity of labor market institutions, educational systems, and social safety nets to adapt. Educational systems that take a decade or more to redesign curricula and retrain teaching workforces are being asked to prepare students for a labor market whose contours in ten years remain genuinely uncertain. Corporate training programs that have historically focused on narrow job-specific skills are being pressured to develop broader cognitive adaptability — critical thinking, digital literacy, collaborative problem-solving — in workforces that may need to navigate multiple career transitions across a working lifetime. [4]", "The deeper philosophical question that AI's labor market impact raises is one that economists' models and policymakers' frameworks are poorly equipped to address directly: what is work for? If AI systems become capable of performing a sufficiently wide range of cognitive and creative tasks, the institution of paid labor as the primary mechanism through which most people derive identity, social connection, economic security, and a sense of contribution to the larger community will require fundamental reconsideration. This is not a prediction of imminent technological unemployment but a recognition that the assumptions embedded in contemporary labor market institutions reflect a particular historical moment — one that is already passing — and that building the conceptual and institutional foundations for what comes next is a task that cannot responsibly be deferred until the transition is already complete."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the central argument of the passage?", options: ["AI differs from previous automation waves in its cognitive scope, and its labor market effects — which are unequal and structurally disruptive — demand urgent and multidimensional responses.", "The primary challenge of AI for the workforce is a skills gap that can be resolved through expanded corporate training programs.", "AI will inevitably cause mass unemployment and governments must introduce universal basic income without delay.", "Historical evidence clearly shows that AI, like all previous automation technologies, will ultimately create more jobs than it destroys."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"augment\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["enhance and extend the capabilities of", "gradually replace through incremental task reassignment", "retrain and redeploy toward higher-value functions", "monitor and evaluate through automated performance systems"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is cited as an example of how AI is currently being used to assist professionals?", options: ["Screening diagnostic images and flagging anomalous transactions in finance and medicine", "Replacing senior legal counsel in courtroom proceedings involving complex contract disputes", "Conducting patient consultations and issuing prescriptions under remote physician supervision", "Generating financial forecasts that remove the need for human analysts in investment banking"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What does the author imply in paragraph 3 about the effect of AI on early-career professionals?", options: ["Early-career professionals are largely unaffected by AI because their work primarily involves interpersonal skills that current systems cannot replicate.", "AI tools used in professional settings are currently too unreliable to be trusted with the kinds of tasks that junior professionals typically perform.", "The compression of junior-level work by AI may deprive new professionals of the formative experiences through which tacit knowledge and contextual judgment are developed.", "AI creates ideal conditions for early-career professionals by automating repetitive tasks and freeing them to focus on higher-level work sooner."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a proposed policy response to AI-driven labor market disruption?", options: ["Universal basic income", "Mandatory profit-sharing schemes that distribute AI productivity gains to affected workers", "Mandatory reduction of working hours across all industries to share available employment", "Robot taxes on firms that automate previously human-performed work"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"radical\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["representing fundamental and far-reaching structural change", "politically popular but economically impractical", "applicable only to specific industries rather than the broader economy", "experimental and not yet supported by empirical research"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the author mean by the \"hollowing out\" of middle-skill, middle-wage occupations?", options: ["The replacement of human managers in mid-level corporate roles by AI systems capable of supervising automated workflows.", "The erosion of occupations that have historically formed the economic foundation of the middle class, as AI disproportionately displaces workers in that middle band of skills and wages.", "The gradual migration of middle-income workers into lower-wage service sector jobs as manufacturing declines globally.", "The decline in the number of workers who qualify for middle-wage positions due to rising educational requirements across most industries."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What tension does the author identify by combining information from paragraphs 5 and 6?", options: ["The mismatch between the pace at which AI is advancing and the much slower rate at which labor institutions, education systems, and social safety nets are able to adapt.", "The tension between the interests of large technology corporations and the regulatory objectives of national governments seeking to control AI deployment.", "The conflict between workers who wish to preserve existing jobs and employers who argue that automation is essential for remaining globally competitive.", "The disagreement between economists who favor market-based solutions and policymakers who prefer regulatory intervention in managing AI's workforce effects."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following best characterizes the author's attitude toward the long-term implications of AI for human labor?", options: ["Analytically cautious, raising fundamental questions about the purpose of work and the adequacy of current institutions without offering definitive predictions.", "Resigned, suggesting that the disruption caused by AI is inevitable and that little can be done to shape its trajectory through policy.", "Alarmist, as the author predicts imminent mass unemployment and the collapse of existing labor market institutions.", "Optimistic, as the author believes AI will ultimately generate prosperity that is broadly shared across all income levels."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to the passage? \"The question of whether this historical pattern will hold for AI — whether new categories of work will emerge quickly enough and at sufficient scale to absorb displaced workers — is one that even the most sophisticated economic models cannot yet answer with confidence.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-089", num: 89, title: "Đề 089", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Why Birds Often Collide with Buildings", paragraphs: ["Every year, hundreds of millions of birds die after flying into windows and glass walls of buildings around the world. This problem is far more serious than most people realize. In the United States alone, researchers estimate that between 300 million and one billion birds are killed this way each year, making window collisions one of the leading causes of bird death caused by human activity. Despite the large scale of the problem, many people are unaware that it exists — mainly because the birds that die are often small, and their bodies quickly disappear, carried away by cats, foxes, or other scavengers.", "The main reason birds crash into windows is that glass is transparent . When a bird looks at a window, it does not see a solid barrier. Instead, it may see a reflection of the sky or the surrounding trees. The bird believes it is looking at open space and flies forward without hesitation. This is especially common in buildings that have large glass walls or windows that face gardens or parks. The natural scenery reflected in the glass tricks the bird into thinking the path ahead is clear.", "There are certain times of year when collisions happen more often. During spring and autumn, millions of birds migrate across long distances, traveling between their winter and summer homes. Many of these birds travel at night, guided by the stars and the Earth's magnetic field. However, the bright lights of city buildings can confuse them during these nighttime journeys. Attracted to the light, birds circle around tall illuminated buildings and sometimes fly directly into the glass. Research has shown that collisions increase significantly during migration periods compared to non-migration months.", "Not all buildings cause the same level of danger. Buildings with large, uninterrupted glass surfaces pose the greatest risk to birds, particularly when they are surrounded by trees or placed near water, since these environments are reflected clearly in the glass. Lower buildings — typically those under four stories tall — actually cause more total bird deaths than skyscrapers, simply because there are far more of them in most cities. Suburban homes with garden-facing windows are responsible for a surprisingly high number of collisions each year, a fact that many homeowners find difficult to believe.", "Fortunately, there are practical steps that people and building designers can take to reduce the number of collisions. One of the most effective methods is to apply a pattern of dots, stripes, or lines to the outside surface of glass. When these markings are spaced no more than five centimeters apart, birds can recognize the glass as a solid object and adjust their flight path to avoid it. Special ultraviolet-reflective glass is another option — birds can see ultraviolet light, which humans cannot, so this type of glass appears marked to birds while looking clear to people. Keeping indoor plants away from windows and turning off unnecessary lights at night during migration seasons also helps reduce the number of incidents.", "Bird collisions are not simply a matter of individual animals being harmed. Many of the species most affected — including certain types of warblers, thrushes, and sparrows — are already experiencing population declines due to habitat loss and climate change. When large numbers of these birds die during migration, it can affect the overall health of bird populations in ways that take decades to fully understand. Conservation groups around the world are now working with architects, city planners, and building owners to design and retrofit structures that are safer for wildlife. Awareness of the problem is growing, and with simple changes in design and behavior, humans can significantly reduce the impact of buildings on the birds that share our environment."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["The dangers of bright city lights for wildlife living in urban areas", "The migration patterns of birds during spring and autumn seasons", "How architects design buildings that are friendly to the natural environment", "Why birds crash into glass buildings and how this problem can be reduced"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how many birds are estimated to die from window collisions in the United States each year?", options: ["More than two billion", "Between 300 million and one billion", "Exactly 500 million", "Fewer than 100 million"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do birds fly into glass windows without stopping?", options: ["Glass reflects images of open sky or trees, making birds think the way ahead is clear.", "Young birds that have not yet completed their first migration lack experience with glass.", "Birds cannot hear the sounds inside buildings that would warn them of danger.", "Birds are attracted to the warmth produced by large glass surfaces in sunlight."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do more bird collisions occur during spring and autumn?", options: ["More bird species build nests near buildings during warm weather periods.", "Large numbers of birds are traveling between seasonal habitats, often flying at night near lit buildings.", "City workers clean windows more frequently during spring and autumn, making them more dangerous.", "Glass surfaces become more reflective during these seasons due to lower sunlight angles."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which type of building causes the greatest total number of bird deaths?", options: ["Office buildings with glass walls that face rivers or lakes", "Skyscrapers located in the center of large cities", "Suburban homes built in areas with no trees or gardens nearby", "Short buildings of four stories or fewer, because there are so many of them"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the bright lights shining from city buildings", "the researchers who study bird behavior during migration", "the birds that are migrating at night", "the stars that birds use to navigate at night"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way to reduce bird collisions with buildings?", options: ["Turning off lights that are not needed during bird migration seasons", "Placing patterns of dots or stripes on the outer surface of glass", "Using glass that reflects ultraviolet light visible only to birds", "Painting windows with dark colors to make them less transparent"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"transparent\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["clear enough to see through, allowing light to pass", "hard and resistant to breaking under impact", "extremely bright and difficult to look at directly", "smooth and reflective on both sides of the surface"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"retrofit\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to test a building's safety by observing wildlife behavior nearby", "to legally require all new buildings to follow wildlife protection rules", "to demolish old structures and replace them with new ones", "to add new features or improvements to something that already exists"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the problem of bird collisions with buildings?", options: ["Pessimistic, suggesting the problem is too large to be effectively addressed", "Indifferent, presenting the facts without expressing concern for birds or the environment", "Critical, blaming building owners for deliberately ignoring the welfare of wildlife", "Concerned but hopeful, acknowledging the seriousness of the issue while pointing to practical solutions"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Global Success of Li Ziqi on YouTube", paragraphs: ["In a media landscape dominated by fast-paced entertainment, celebrity gossip, and viral challenges, the YouTube channel of a young Chinese woman named Li Ziqi stands as one of the most remarkable exceptions. With no spoken commentary, no English subtitles, and no explanation of what she is doing or why, Li Ziqi's videos have attracted hundreds of millions of views worldwide. Her channel, which focuses on traditional Chinese crafts, rural cooking, and the rhythms of life in the Chinese countryside, has built one of the largest international followings of any non-English-language creator on the platform.", "Li Ziqi was born in 1990 in Pingwu County, in the mountainous Sichuan province of southwestern China. Her early life was marked by hardship: she lost her father at a young age, and her mother left the family shortly afterward. She was raised by her paternal grandparents in a small rural village, far from the opportunities of urban life. As a teenager, she moved to a city to find work, spending several years doing low-wage jobs in restaurants and shops. When her grandmother fell ill, Li Ziqi returned to the village to care for her . It was during this period that she began filming short videos for a Chinese social media platform, teaching herself both the traditional skills she filmed and the technical process of video production.", "The content of her YouTube videos is unlike almost anything else on the platform. Li Ziqi grinds her own flour, dyes fabric using plants she has cultivated herself, builds furniture from bamboo, and prepares elaborate traditional meals using ingredients she has grown, harvested, or foraged from the surrounding landscape. Each video is filmed with high production quality — carefully composed shots, natural lighting, and a gentle musical soundtrack — yet the skills and lifestyle it depicts are those of centuries-old rural traditions. The combination of stunning visual beauty and deeply rooted cultural practice has proven to be compelling to audiences in countries as diverse as France, Brazil, India, and Japan.", "The reasons for her global appeal are the subject of considerable discussion. Some media analysts argue that her videos satisfy a widespread longing for simplicity and connection with nature in an era of urban stress and digital overload. Others point to the universal language of food and craft, suggesting that the pleasure of watching someone transform raw materials into beautiful, finished things requires no translation. A smaller number of commentators have raised questions about the extent to which her lifestyle is authentic — noting that the level of production involved in her videos suggests a professional team rather than a single individual working alone — though Li Ziqi herself has never claimed otherwise.", "Her success has also attracted significant attention in China, where she has been held up by state media as an example of positive cultural promotion. Officials have praised her work for \"showcasing the charm of Chinese culture\" to international audiences at a time when China's global image is a matter of considerable political sensitivity. This official endorsement has led some Western commentators to view her channel with a degree of suspicion, interpreting it as a form of soft power rather than genuine personal expression. The debate reflects broader tensions in how cultural content that crosses national boundaries is understood and evaluated.", "Whatever the nature of her creative process or the political context surrounding her fame, Li Ziqi's impact on her immediate community has been tangible . She has invested in local agricultural projects, supported craftspeople in her village, and helped bring economic attention to a rural area that had little previous visibility. In 2021, she entered the Guinness World Records as the YouTube creator with the most subscribers for a Chinese-language channel. Her channel was temporarily suspended that same year amid a widely reported business dispute with her management company, leaving over 16 million subscribers without new content for more than a year — a pause that only seemed to intensify public interest in her eventual return.", "Li Ziqi's story raises questions that extend beyond her individual career. In a global media environment in which attention is typically rewarded to the loud, the fast, and the provocative, her quiet, slow, and deeply traditional content has found an audience that neither she nor the platforms she uses could have predicted. Whether her success represents a lasting shift in what viewers seek from digital media, or simply an exceptional case unlikely to be repeated, remains to be seen. What is certain is that her channel has demonstrated that beauty, craft, and cultural authenticity can travel across languages and borders without losing their power."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To argue that Li Ziqi's channel is a form of Chinese government propaganda", "To compare the content style of Chinese YouTube creators with those from Western countries", "To examine Li Ziqi's rise to global fame and the wider questions her success raises", "To explain how YouTube's recommendation algorithm helped Li Ziqi gain subscribers"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what caused Li Ziqi to return to her village after working in the city?", options: ["She decided to start making videos for a Chinese social media platform.", "Her grandmother became sick and needed someone to look after her.", "She lost her job and could no longer afford to live in the city.", "She was invited back by local officials to help promote rural tourism."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes the content of Li Ziqi's YouTube videos?", options: ["Fast-paced cooking tutorials aimed at teaching young Chinese people traditional recipes", "Documentary-style interviews with rural craftspeople and farmers across Sichuan province", "Visually rich recordings of traditional crafts, farming, and food preparation in a rural Chinese setting", "Travel vlogs showing the contrast between modern Chinese cities and the countryside"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what record did Li Ziqi achieve in 2021?", options: ["She was recognized as the YouTube creator with the most subscribers for a Chinese-language channel.", "She became the most-watched food content creator on any video platform in the world.", "She received an official government award for promoting Chinese culture internationally.", "She became the first non-English-speaking creator to reach 100 million subscribers on YouTube."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what explanation do some media analysts offer for Li Ziqi's international appeal?", options: ["Her use of English subtitles allows non-Chinese audiences to follow her instructions easily.", "Her channel benefits from YouTube's algorithm, which actively promotes non-English content globally.", "Her content is promoted heavily by the Chinese government through official media channels.", "Her videos tap into a broad desire for simplicity and connection with nature among urban audiences."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following activities is NOT mentioned as something Li Ziqi does in her videos?", options: ["Dyeing fabric with plants she has grown herself", "Building furniture using bamboo", "Weaving cloth from sheep's wool", "Grinding her own flour from grain"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"her\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["Li Ziqi's paternal grandmother, who had raised her in the village", "Li Ziqi's mother, who had left the family when Li Ziqi was young", "Li Ziqi herself, who had become exhausted from working in the city", "A neighbor in the village who needed medical assistance"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"compelling\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["difficult to understand without background knowledge of Chinese culture", "simple and easy to copy, encouraging others to try similar activities", "controversial and likely to produce strong disagreement among viewers", "powerfully attractive and holding the attention of the audience"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"tangible\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["real and clearly visible through specific evidence or results", "small in scale but emotionally meaningful to those involved", "unexpected and difficult to explain given the circumstances", "widely debated and not yet agreed upon by researchers"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the debate over whether Li Ziqi's channel represents soft power?", options: ["Li Ziqi has publicly denied any connection between her channel and China's international image strategies.", "Western audiences have stopped watching Li Ziqi's videos because of concerns about political influence.", "The passage confirms that Li Ziqi works directly with the Chinese government to plan her video content.", "The fact that state media praised her work does not by itself prove that her content is politically motivated."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Expanding Role of Technology in Modern Education", paragraphs: ["Over the past two decades, digital technology has moved from the margins of classroom practice to its very center, reshaping how knowledge is delivered, how students engage with learning, and how educators measure academic progress. What began as a modest experiment — placing computers in school laboratories and occasionally using the internet to supplement textbook content — has grown into a comprehensive transformation that touches every level of education, from primary schools in rural communities to postgraduate programs at elite research universities. The pace of this transformation accelerated dramatically during the COVID-19 pandemic, when school closures forced educators and students worldwide to adopt remote learning tools with little preparation and no guarantee of success.", "The most immediate and visible change has been the proliferation of online learning platforms. Services such as Khan Academy, Coursera, and Google Classroom have made it possible for students to access instructional content, complete assignments, and receive feedback from instructors without ever entering a physical classroom. [1] For learners in geographically isolated regions, where qualified teachers are scarce and well-resourced schools are hours away, these platforms have provided access to educational content that was previously unavailable. Studies conducted in several developing countries have found that students who regularly use structured online platforms show measurable improvements in literacy and numeracy skills, though researchers consistently note that access to a reliable internet connection and a functioning device remains a significant barrier for many households.", "Adaptive learning technology represents one of the most discussed innovations in the field. Unlike traditional instruction, which delivers the same content to all students simultaneously, adaptive systems use algorithms to monitor each learner's responses and adjust the difficulty and focus of subsequent material accordingly. [2] A student who answers several mathematics questions incorrectly will be automatically redirected to foundational concepts, while a student performing at a high level will be presented with more challenging problems. Proponents argue that this approach mirrors the responsiveness of a skilled private tutor without the associated cost, potentially democratizing access to personalized instruction on a large scale. Critics, however, caution that algorithmic systems can reinforce existing gaps if they are trained on data that does not reflect the full diversity of learners.", "The integration of technology into assessment has also attracted considerable attention. Digital testing platforms allow for the rapid scoring of objective questions and the immediate delivery of performance data to both students and teachers. Some institutions have moved toward continuous digital assessment — replacing annual or semester-end examinations with ongoing monitoring of student activity, quiz scores, and project submissions. Supporters of this model argue that it produces a more accurate and comprehensive picture of a student's abilities than a single high-stakes examination can provide. [3] Opponents point to concerns about data privacy, the potential for students to feel under constant surveillance, and the risk that continuous monitoring may disadvantage students who perform inconsistently due to personal or family circumstances rather than genuine gaps in knowledge.", "Perhaps the most contentious debate surrounds the use of artificial intelligence in educational settings. AI-powered tools can now generate personalized study plans, flag potential learning difficulties before they become serious, and even provide automated feedback on student writing. Some institutions have begun experimenting with AI tutoring systems capable of engaging students in extended dialogue about academic subjects — a development that raises profound questions about the future role of human teachers. Educators and policymakers differ sharply on whether these tools should be welcomed as powerful supplements to human instruction or regarded with caution as threats to the relational dimension of teaching that many consider irreplaceable. [4]", "A parallel concern relates to equity. The benefits of educational technology are not distributed evenly. Schools in wealthier districts tend to have faster internet connections, more devices per student, and better-trained teachers who can integrate technology effectively into their practice. Schools serving lower-income communities often lack not only the hardware and connectivity required to use advanced platforms but also the technical support staff needed to maintain them. Unless these structural disparities are actively addressed by governments and education authorities, the proliferation of educational technology risks widening, rather than narrowing, the gap between students from different socioeconomic backgrounds.", "The evidence on whether technology genuinely improves educational outcomes remains mixed. Meta-analyses of large numbers of studies suggest that the impact of technology on student achievement is modest and highly dependent on how tools are implemented, by whom, and in what institutional context. Technology, the research consensus implies, is neither a solution in itself nor merely a distraction — it is a tool whose value is determined by the quality of the educational practice surrounding its use. As schools continue to invest in digital infrastructure and platforms, the most important question may not be how much technology is present in the classroom, but how thoughtfully it is being used."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central argument of the passage regarding technology in education?", options: ["Technology is a tool whose educational value depends fundamentally on how thoughtfully it is applied rather than on its mere presence.", "Artificial intelligence will eventually replace human teachers in most educational settings.", "Digital technology has already solved most of the problems facing modern educational systems worldwide.", "The benefits of educational technology are outweighed by the risks it poses to student privacy and wellbeing."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what specific benefit have online learning platforms provided to students in remote areas?", options: ["They have connected isolated students directly with university professors for one-to-one tutoring sessions.", "They have given students access to educational content that was previously out of reach due to lack of local teachers and resources.", "They have funded the construction of new school buildings in geographically isolated communities.", "They have supplied physical textbooks and study materials to students who lack access to bookshops."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does an adaptive learning system respond when a student repeatedly answers mathematics questions incorrectly?", options: ["It increases the number of questions the student must answer before advancing.", "It alerts the student's teacher and recommends face-to-face intervention.", "It pauses the student's progress and requires a written explanation of each incorrect answer.", "It automatically directs the student back to more foundational material to address the gap."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do supporters of continuous digital assessment argue in its favor?", options: ["It provides a fuller and more accurate picture of a student's abilities than a single high-stakes exam can offer.", "It eliminates the need for teachers to give students any written feedback on their work.", "It saves schools significant money by removing the cost of printing examination papers.", "It ensures that all students are evaluated according to the same standard regardless of their background."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do some educators regard AI tutoring tools with caution?", options: ["Research has shown that students using AI tutors consistently perform worse than those without them.", "AI tutoring systems are currently too expensive for most schools to afford.", "AI systems generate study plans that are too advanced for average students to follow.", "They are concerned that AI tools may replace the human relationships that are central to effective teaching."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a concern raised by opponents of continuous digital assessment?", options: ["The risk that students may feel constantly watched and monitored", "The likelihood that teachers will lose the skills needed to design their own assessments", "The possibility that students from difficult personal circumstances may be unfairly penalized", "The danger of sensitive student performance data being inadequately protected"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the critics who warn about the limitations of adaptive learning systems", "the algorithmic systems used in adaptive learning technology", "the data sets collected from students across various educational institutions", "the existing gaps in achievement between different groups of learners"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"democratizing\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["making something affordable by reducing its production cost significantly", "allowing individuals to vote on which educational programs they prefer", "making something available and accessible to a much wider range of people", "converting a private service into one managed by a government body"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"disparities\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["financial penalties imposed on schools that fail to meet government standards", "gradual improvements in outcomes achieved over a long period of time", "noticeable differences in conditions or resources between groups", "disagreements between educators about the best methods of teaching"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This flexibility is particularly valuable in large, heterogeneous classrooms where a single instructional pace inevitably leaves some students behind.'", options: ["[1]", "[3]", "[4]", "[2]"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Remarkable Bite Forces of Powerful Animals", paragraphs: ["Among the many metrics by which scientists measure the physical capabilities of animals, bite force has attracted particular attention — not merely as a curiosity, but as a window into evolutionary history, dietary adaptation, and ecological competition. Measured in pounds per square inch (PSI) or newtons, bite force reflects the combined output of jaw musculature, skeletal architecture, and tooth morphology, making it one of the most integrative indicators of an animal's relationship with its food source and, by extension, its niche in a given ecosystem. [1] What the raw numbers reveal, when examined carefully, is that the capacity to generate extraordinary crushing or gripping force has evolved independently across a remarkable range of taxonomic groups — a phenomenon that evolutionary biologists describe as convergent evolution.", "The saltwater crocodile, the largest living reptile, is frequently cited as the animal with the most powerful bite on Earth among vertebrates. Bite force measurements obtained through the use of custom-built bite transducers — instruments designed to withstand the mechanical load of a crocodilian jaw — have recorded values exceeding 3,700 pounds per square inch in large adult specimens. [2] This extraordinary force enables the saltwater crocodile to seize and immobilize prey as large as water buffalo and to resist the muscular thrashing of animals attempting to escape. Paradoxically, however, the muscles responsible for opening the jaw are comparatively weak, a fact exploited by wildlife handlers who restrain crocodiles using little more than a firm grip and, occasionally, a simple rubber band.", "Among mammals, the spotted hyena commands attention as a biting specialist whose dentition and jaw anatomy are calibrated almost entirely for processing bone. With a recorded bite force of approximately 1,100 PSI, the spotted hyena is capable of crushing the femur of a large ungulate — a feat beyond the reach of most carnivores, including lions and leopards, which typically abandon skeletal remains after consuming softer tissue. This capacity for osteophagy, or bone consumption, gives the hyena access to a nutritional resource — the marrow and minerals locked within cortical bone — that competitors cannot efficiently exploit, reducing direct competition and providing a reliable food source during periods of scarcity.", "The snapping turtle, an unassuming freshwater reptile of North America, offers a striking example of disproportionate bite force relative to body size. Although the common snapping turtle weighs no more than 35 pounds in most adult specimens, its bite force can reach values approaching 1,000 PSI when measured under standardized laboratory conditions. The alligator snapping turtle, a larger and rarer relative, has been the subject of considerable popular exaggeration, with unverified claims of bite forces exceeding 2,500 PSI appearing regularly in lay publications; measured values from rigorous studies are considerably more modest, though still impressive for a freshwater invertebrate predator of its mass. [3]", "Insects, despite their diminutive scale, are not absent from discussions of bite force when measurements are adjusted for body mass. The trap-jaw ant, a species native to Central and South America, possesses mandibles capable of closing at speeds of up to 145 miles per hour — among the fastest recorded movements of any biological structure. While the absolute force generated is modest by vertebrate standards, the mechanical energy produced relative to the ant's body weight is extraordinary, and the strike is used not only for prey capture but also, remarkably, as a propulsive mechanism: an ant struck against a hard surface will launch itself into the air to escape predators, a behavior that has no precise parallel among vertebrates. [4]", "The evolution of extreme bite force is not without trade-offs. In species where jaw musculature has been maximized for crushing force, other cranial structures have often been correspondingly modified — sometimes at the expense of sensory organs, brain volume, or the capacity for jaw mobility that broader dietary flexibility would require. The skull of the spotted hyena, for example, is so heavily reinforced to transmit bite forces that it affords relatively little room for the expansion of brain tissue compared with similarly sized carnivores. These trade-offs suggest that extraordinary bite force is an evolutionary specialization rather than an unqualified advantage — a solution to a specific ecological problem that simultaneously forecloses other adaptive possibilities.", "What emerges from a comparative survey of bite forces across the animal kingdom is less a simple hierarchy of power than a complex map of ecological niches, evolutionary pressures, and biomechanical constraints. The animals that bite hardest are not necessarily the most dominant in their ecosystems; rather, they are those for whom a particular feeding strategy — bone-cracking, shell-crushing, or prey immobilization — has become so central to survival that natural selection has driven jaw mechanics to exceptional extremes. Understanding bite force, in this sense, is ultimately less about cataloguing impressive statistics and more about appreciating the intricate ways in which physical capability and ecological circumstance shape each other over evolutionary time."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central point the author makes about bite force across animal species?", options: ["Bite force measurements are unreliable because laboratory conditions rarely reflect real hunting situations.", "The development of powerful bite forces has been limited to reptiles and mammals due to their skeletal architecture.", "Extreme bite force consistently correlates with dominance and reproductive success within an ecosystem.", "Extraordinary bite force is an evolutionary specialization shaped by specific ecological pressures, not a mark of universal superiority."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what unexpected characteristic of the saltwater crocodile does the author highlight alongside its powerful bite?", options: ["Despite its crushing jaw strength, the muscles that open its jaw are relatively weak.", "The instruments used to measure its bite force frequently break, making accurate data difficult to obtain.", "Its teeth are surprisingly fragile and must be replaced several times throughout its lifetime.", "Its bite force is strongest when measured on land rather than in water."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the spotted hyena's ability to consume bone give it an ecological advantage over predators such as lions?", options: ["It gives hyenas the ability to store food underground in a form that resists decomposition.", "It makes hyenas faster runners, as a bone-rich diet strengthens their muscular systems.", "It allows hyenas to survive on food sources that other carnivores cannot efficiently use, reducing direct competition.", "It enables hyenas to hunt prey that lions cannot physically overpower."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, in what way does the trap-jaw ant use its mandible strike beyond simply capturing prey?", options: ["It uses the recoil from striking a hard surface to propel itself away from predators.", "It applies rapid mandible strikes to dig tunnels through compacted soil more efficiently.", "It uses repeated strikes to generate heat that helps incubate the colony's eggs.", "It uses the strike to communicate territorial boundaries to rival colonies."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the skull structure of the spotted hyena illustrate about the trade-offs of extreme bite force?", options: ["Bone-crushing specialists develop thinner skull walls to allow more precise control of jaw movements.", "Maximizing bite force through skeletal reinforcement can come at the cost of reduced brain capacity.", "A heavily reinforced skull increases sensory capability but reduces overall running speed.", "The skull adaptations that produce strong biting make hyenas more vulnerable to predators from above."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT stated or implied as a characteristic or advantage of the spotted hyena?", options: ["Its bite force is sufficient to crack the large bones of ungulates", "Its dietary specialization reduces the degree to which it must compete directly with lions", "It uses its bone-crushing ability to cache food for later consumption underground", "It can access bone marrow that most other carnivores leave behind"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"its\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the standardized laboratory condition used to measure bite force", "the common snapping turtle described at the start of the paragraph", "the bite force measurement recorded in lay publications", "the alligator snapping turtle, which is larger and rarer"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"calibrated\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["finely adjusted and structured to serve a particular function", "measured precisely using specialized scientific instruments", "made visually distinctive to signal threat to rival predators", "gradually strengthened through repeated use over many years"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"forecloses\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["permanently eliminates or makes unavailable certain possibilities", "delays the development of an ability until environmental conditions improve", "transfers a biological advantage from one species to a closely related one", "creates a protective barrier around a specialized anatomical structure"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Yet despite its fearsome reputation, the crocodile's jaw presents a biomechanical paradox that surprises most observers.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
];
